## Chapter 9: Instruction Selection

Instruction Selection translates the target-independent middle-end IR into a low-level Machine IR (MIR) composed of concrete instructions from the target ISA (e.g., x86-64, AArch64, RISC-V). Because CISC and RISC architectures often provide single instructions that perform multiple primitive IR operations simultaneously (such as x86-64 `lea rax, [rdi + rsi*4 + 16]` or AArch64 `madd` multiply-accumulate), Instruction Selection is formulated as a **Minimum-Cost Tree or DAG Tiling Problem**.

### 9.1 Tree-Pattern Matching & Dynamic Programming (BURS)

Within a basic block, expression trees in the IR can be tiled by a set of **Target Machine Tree Patterns**, where each pattern is a tree grammar production $N \to \text{Pattern}$ paired with an execution cost $c \ge 0$:

| Rule # | Grammar Production | Target x86-64 Instruction | Cost |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | $r \to \text{CONST } c$ | `mov r, c` | $1$ |
| 2 | $r_0 \to \text{ADD}(r_1, r_2)$ | `lea r0, [r1 + r2]` | $1$ |
| 3 | $r_0 \to \text{ADD}(r_1, \text{CONST } c)$ | `add r0, c` | $1$ |
| 4 | $r_0 \to \text{LOAD}(\text{ADD}(r_1, \text{MUL}(r_2, \text{CONST } 4)))$ | `mov r0, [r1 + r2*4]` | $2$ |
| 5 | $r_0 \to \text{LOAD}(r_1)$ | `mov r0, [r1]` | $2$ |
| 6 | $r_0 \to \text{MUL}(r_1, r_2)$ | `imul r0, r2` | $3$ |

#### Maximal Munch vs. Dynamic Programming (Aho-Johnson / BURS)
* **Maximal Munch on Trees:** Starts at the root of the expression tree and greedily matches the largest pattern (most nodes covered) at the root, then recurses on the leaves of the pattern. While fast ($O(N)$), Maximal Munch is **not optimal** when instruction costs are non-uniform!
* **Dynamic Programming (Bottom-Up Rewrite Systems — BURS):** Computes the provably minimum-cost tiling in two linear passes over the expression tree:
  1. **Bottom-Up Pass (Post-Order):** For every tree node $u$ and every grammar non-terminal $N$ (e.g., `Reg`, `AddrMode`, `Imm`), compute $\text{Cost}[u][N]$ = the minimum cost to evaluate the subtree rooted at $u$ into non-terminal $N$, and $\text{BestRule}[u][N]$ = the production rule that achieved that minimum:
     $$\text{Cost}[u][N] = \min_{(N \to P) \in R} \left( \text{cost}(N \to P) + \sum_{l_i \in \text{Leaves}(P, u)} \text{Cost}[l_i][N_i] \right)$$
  2. **Top-Down Pass (Pre-Order):** Starting at $(\text{root}, \text{Reg})$, look up $P = \text{BestRule}[\text{root}][\text{Reg}]$, recursively emit code for the leaves $(l_i, N_i)$ of pattern $P$ at `root`, and then emit the machine instruction for $P$.

*(Note: Tools like `twig`, `iburg`, and LLVM's TableGen precompute or drive this matching across DAGs—whereLLVM's `SelectionDAGISel` extends tree matching to DAGs within a basic block, while `GlobalISel` and `FastISel` provide faster linear passes for `-O0` compilation).*

