## Chapter 12: Interprocedural Analysis, Runtime Systems, & Code Generation

Up to this point, our analyses and transformations have operated intraprocedurally within a single function. When a function calls another function `foo(p)` or accesses pointers, an intraprocedural pass without interprocedural knowledge must make worst-case assumptions: that `foo` might overwrite any global variable or heap address reachable from `p`.

### 12.1 Interprocedural Analysis & Pointer/Alias Analysis

#### 12.1.1 Call Graph Construction & Devirtualization
A **Call Graph** $CG = (F, E)$ is a directed multigraph where nodes are functions and an edge $f \xrightarrow{c} g$ represents a call site $c$ inside $f$ invoking $g$.
* For direct calls, the target $g$ is statically known.
* For indirect calls (C function pointers) or object-oriented virtual method dispatches (`obj->vptr[idx](obj)`), the compiler narrows down the set of possible targets using **Class Hierarchy Analysis (CHA)** or **Rapid Type Analysis (RTA)** (which restricts CHA to classes that are actually instantiated in the program). If the target set is reduced to a singleton $\{g\}$, the compiler performs **Devirtualization**, replacing the indirect vtable lookup with a direct call (which can then be **Inlined**!).

#### 12.1.2 Andersen's Inclusion-Based vs. Steensgaard's Unification-Based Points-To Analysis
Given two pointers $p$ and $q$, **Alias Analysis** answers whether $p$ and $q$ can point to the same memory location ($\text{NoAlias}$, $\text{MayAlias}$, $\text{MustAlias}$). Flow-insensitive Points-To Analysis computes an abstract points-to set $\text{ pts}(p) \subseteq \text{AllocSites}$ for every pointer $p$:

| Statement Type | Andersen's Subset Constraint ($\subseteq$) | Steensgaard's Equality Constraint ($=$) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Address-Of (`p = &x`)** | $l_x \in \text{pts}(p)$ | $l_x \in \text{pts}(p)$ |
| **Copy (`p = q`)** | $\text{pts}(q) \subseteq \text{pts}(p)$ | $\text{pts}(q) = \text{pts}(p)$ |
| **Load (`p = *q`)** | $\forall v \in \text{pts}(q), \; \text{pts}(v) \subseteq \text{pts}(p)$ | $\forall v \in \text{pts}(q), \; \text{pts}(v) = \text{pts}(p)$ |
| **Store (`*p = q`)** | $\forall v \in \text{pts}(p), \; \text{pts}(q) \subseteq \text{pts}(v)$ | $\forall v \in \text{pts}(p), \; \text{pts}(q) = \text{pts}(v)$ |

* **Steensgaard's Algorithm (1996):** Treats assignment directionally symmetrically ($\text{pts}(q) = \text{pts}(p)$), merging points-to sets via Disjoint-Set (Union-Find) in nearly linear **$O(N \alpha(N))$** time, at the cost of lower precision (if `p = &a; p = &b; q = &a;`, Steensgaard merges `a` and `b` so `q` spuriously points to `b` too!).
* **Andersen's Algorithm (1994):** Preserves directional subset constraints ($\text{pts}(q) \subseteq \text{pts}(p)$), computing the dynamic transitive closure of the constraint graph in **$O(N^3)$** worst-case time with significantly higher precision.

---

### 12.2 Calling Conventions & Stack Frame Layout (System V AMD64 ABI)

To ensure binary interoperability across separately compiled object files and libraries, compilers strictly adhere to a platform **Application Binary Interface (ABI)**. On Linux, macOS, and BSD x86-64 systems, the **System V AMD64 ABI** specifies:

1. **Argument Passing:**
   * The first **6 integer/pointer arguments** are passed in registers: **`%rdi`, `%rsi`, `%rdx`, `%rcx`, `%r8`, `%r9`**.
   * The first **8 floating-point/vector arguments** are passed in **`%xmm0` through `%xmm7`**.
   * Additional arguments ($7\text{th}$ integer argument onward) are pushed onto the stack from right to left.
2. **Return Values:**
   * Integer/pointer return values up to 64 bits are returned in **`%rax`** (up to 128 bits in `%rdx:%rax`). Floating-point return values use **`%xmm0`**.
3. **Caller-Saved vs. Callee-Saved Registers:**
   * **Callee-Saved (Non-Volatile):** **`%rbx`, `%rbp`, `%r12`, `%r13`, `%r14`, `%r15`** (plus `%rsp`). If a function uses any of these registers, its prologue must save them to the stack and its epilogue must restore them before returning.
   * **Caller-Saved (Volatile / Scratch):** **`%rax`, `%rcx`, `%rdx`, `%rsi`, `%rdi`, `%r8`, `%r9`, `%r10`, `%r11`**, and all `%xmm` registers. A callee may freely overwrite these without restoring them.
4. **Stack Alignment & Red Zone:**
   * **16-Byte Alignment Invariant:** Immediately *before* executing a `call` instruction, the stack pointer `%rsp` **must be aligned to a 16-byte boundary** (`%rsp % 16 == 0`). Because `call` pushes an 8-byte return address onto the stack, on entry to the callee `%rsp % 16 == 8`. Pushing `%rbp` (`push rbp`, 8 bytes) restores `%rsp % 16 == 0`!
   * **The Red Zone:** Leaf functions on System V AMD64 may use the 128 bytes immediately below `%rsp` (`[rsp - 128]` to `[rsp - 8]`) without adjusting `%rsp`, as the OS kernel guarantees signal handlers will not clobber this 128-byte window (Note: Windows x64 ABI has *no* Red Zone and passes the first 4 arguments in `%rcx, %rdx, %r8, %r9` with a 32-byte shadow space).

```
High Memory
┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Argument 8 (if > 6 args)                 │ [rbp + 24]
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Argument 7 (if > 6 args)                 │ [rbp + 16]
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Return Address (pushed by 'call')        │ [rbp + 8]
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Saved Caller %rbp (pushed by Prologue)   │ ◀── %rbp (16-byte aligned!)
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Saved Callee-Save Registers (%rbx, %r12) │ [rbp - 8], [rbp - 16]
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Local Variables & Spill Slots            │ [rbp - 24] ...
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Alignment Padding (to keep %rsp % 16==0) │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Outgoing Stack Arguments for Callees     │ ◀── %rsp (16-byte aligned before 'call'!)
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 128-Byte Red Zone (Leaf Functions Only)  │ [rsp - 128 .. rsp - 1]
└──────────────────────────────────────────┘
Low Memory
```

---

### 12.3 Garbage Collection & Compiler Support (Safe Points & Stack Maps)

In managed languages (Java, Go, C#, JavaScript, OCaml), an **Exact (Precise) Moving Garbage Collector** must pause execution threads and inspect every live pointer on the thread stacks and in physical CPU registers (**Root Set Enumeration**), updating those pointers if the referenced heap objects are relocated.

How does a Moving GC know whether the 64-bit value currently in register `%rax` is an integer `0x7fff1040` or a heap pointer? It relies on cooperation from the compiler back-end:
1. **GC Safe Points (Poll Sites):** A thread cannot be suspended for GC at an arbitrary machine instruction (e.g., mid-way through initializing a newly allocated object header). Instead, the compiler emits explicit **Safe Point Polls** (often a single dummy read from a guard page `test eax, [r15_poll_page]`, which traps with `SIGSEGV` in 0 extra branch cycles when the GC unmaps the page!) at function entries, call sites, and **loop back-edges**.
2. **Stack Maps:** For every Safe Point instruction address `PC`, the compiler's register allocator emits a compressed metadata entry in a read-only `.llvm_stackmaps` section recording the exact set of physical registers and `[rbp - offset]` stack slots that hold live GC pointers at that exact `PC`.
3. **Write Barriers (Card Marking / Remembered Sets):** In **Generational Garbage Collectors**, scanning the entire Old Generation to find pointers to the Young Generation during a minor GC would defeat the purpose of generational collection. Instead, whenever compiled code mutates a pointer field in the heap (`*obj_field = ptr`), the compiler emits a fast inline **Card-Table Write Barrier** after the store:
   ```nasm
   mov QWORD PTR [rdi + 16], rsi   ; *obj_field = ptr
   shr rdi, 9                      ; Divide address by 512 (512-byte Card size)
   mov BYTE PTR [r14 + rdi], 0     ; Mark Card dirty in Card Table base %r14
   ```

